and sensationalized and that  it was going to be all over the  newspapers and the television and and  particularly that Jackie Kennedy would  see it so  Life magazine then had it for 12 years  and one of the amazing things that  happened for me when I decided to do  this work was that I went back to Life  magazine and I asked if I could see the  the archives and they just hand it over  the entire written record of the life of  the film at life which were documents  that had never been made public with  anyone so there are all these letters  and  is that really so the whole middle part  of the book really goes through what was  happening inside Life magazine how they  handled the film the various  controversies and of course  Life magazine held the film very tightly  and never showed it as a film to the  American people but during those years  there was the rise of bootleg copies  through various sources and of course  the rise of the conspiracy theories  partly fed by the fact that people  couldn't see the film so an unintended  consequence and then this relationship  between life and the film ended in 1975  when they returned the film to our  family for a dollar and there is a story  behind that which is told in the book  but essentially they were under  tremendous pressure from the public from  other news outlets and others to make it  available and they they couldn't do that  and and treat it tastefully and that was  their conflict so they decided to get  rid of it and they were prepared to  donate it to the National Archives but  my grandmother intervened and I will  leave it at that it's a story that is  told in the book I think many of us  remember the first scene first the still  images in life and and then later the  first moving images on the head although  ravenna show right what was a good night  yeah yeah  good night America so that was so a  bootleg copy of the film was shown to  the American public for the first time  in 1975 April 1975 just as these  negotiations were going on and that was  more or less the final straw for Life  magazine because part of their their  sort of terms of their contract were  that they had to defend the copyright to  the film and prevent it from being used  in ways that were not sanctioned and  here it was on Geraldo was out it was  over there was nothing to be done about  it and so at that point they just were  ready to be rid of it and I can't say  that I blame them another question  somebody else  you know one of the please gather your  question and head to that mic can you  talk a little bit about how maybe your  grandfather's immigrant experience  played a role in his reaction to what  happened yes thank you  you know my grandfather came to this  country in 1920 as a 15 year old boy  having endured horrendous persecution  and suffering as a as a Jew in Imperial  Russia he had three kid two sisters and  a brother the brother was either killed  or died it's not exactly clear what  happened to him  and they came here in 1920 my  grandfather went to night school to  learn English and then like almost all  the other Jews in his situation went  into the needle trades and and became a  dressmaker and then he and my  grandmother went to Dallas in 1941 and  by the time the assassination happened  he was running a successful dress  manufacturing business but I think his  whole life was shaped by these traumatic  early years and the sense that he didn't  have an education and he was somewhat  insecure as a result of that but I think  in particular when the in the aftermath  of the assassination his his values and  Jewish values in particular I think  largely shaped his sense of what was and  was not appropriate and decent to do  with the film which were immediately in  conflict with American norms already  even in 1963 to say nothing of today  and I think he was very much afraid that  profiting from the film selling it to  Life magazine both would be perceived in  a negative light and that would would  potentially spark anti-semitic reactions  but more than that that it actually was  wrong not just that it might seem like  it was wrong but it might actually be  wrong and so he was really I think  he was torn by that and I think his his  whole perspective and his decision to  sell to Life magazine and then to give  away he gave away $25,000 to the widow  of the police officer who was shot by  Oswald again his way of sort of the  taking of the money kind of reflected  his own financial insecurity and poverty  and fear and the sense that it was  important to sort of take this windfall  even though the taking of it was  complicated but also to give away a  portion of the money in order to assuage  his own complicated sense of guilt and  responsibility for it and that those  basic and and to define the terms of the  deal with Life magazine to try to  mitigate the damage that the film would  cause and I think those basic elements  remained a hundred percent current for  my father when he inherited a much much  more complicated set of decisions in  1975 and through the 70s and 80s and 90s  when he was dealing with the film he I  think always went back to my  grandfather's values as a sort of  guideposts for figuring out how to deal  with the phone even though times had  changed and things were much more  complicated the question yes you  probably don't address this in your book  and it may be far removed from the story  there but to me I can remember in 1963  and it seemed to me our family didn't  have one but a lot of people had movie  cameras did you spend a lot of time  thinking about and there were a lot of  people along that route right there did  you think about why was it just your  grandfather who happened to get the only  film we know of I find that incredible  yeah there were there were 21 other  photographers along the parade route the  end of the parade route and those films  are incredibly valuable and important I  think the thing  about my grandfather's film that is  different is that it it encapsulates the  whole story and it is so clear and it's  and it has this there's an arc to it  which is sort of what I alluded to in  the epilogue that you know the whole  story not only of the Kennedys and their  fall from grace and this tragic event  but the whole story of human life and  what we strive for and what our fate is  it's all there  and it's and that makes it different  from other things and I also think there  are those who have said and I think that  this is true the composition of it I  mean if you just look at this you know  image this bright green grass and the  pink of the first lady's suit and the  black of the limousine there's something  visually that you know now of course we  have this nostalgic relationship to it  but even at the time I think it was  there's really no accounting for it it's  just that is the way that it looked but  it it grabbed people and it never let go  have the other films of Abraham Zapruder  been preserved there yes they have been  preserved so they have they my aunt my  aunt yeah yeah well right step up we  have about three more minutes so we can  do part question  have you actually held the object the  original film no the original film is in  the National Archives and the film was  taken from our family and then in the  1990s by the federal government  precipitating a long struggle to  determine ownership and value and in  fact it's funny because I asked I wrote  to the National Archives after while I  was working on the book to ask if I  could see the original film and there  was a lot of back and forth and hemming  and hawing and then they said no and I  thought well it's really not ours  anymore is it that's it the second part  of the question is this is just touching  the the rabbit-hole is this all can get  into there was a lot of discussion about  two frames of the film being flipped in  the film  the actual artifact being manipulated  yeah just discussed that I did I did get  into the questions of the alteration of  the film which is there's a whole theory  that that rose up sort of more in the  1990s about altering the film the two  frames that you're referring to I  believe are the way that the film was  presented in the Warren Commission  exhibits frames three I believe it was  three thirteen and three fourteen were  shown frame three thirteen is the  critical frame that shows the head wound  to the president they were printed in  reverse order which was either just the  most unbelievably dunderheaded thing  that somebody did or it was something  worse than that but it doesn't really I  mean so it was very dubious that that  happened and there were also frames that  were damaged by the Life magazine people  in the weekend of the assassination when  they were rushing to go to print with  the first issue of images of the film  and six frames were damaged and spliced  out but life made the mistake of not  coming public with that until much later  which led to an enormous amount of  speculation that that the film had been  altered and then there's a larger  alteration theory which I won't get into  now because of the time but that I went  into and a great length in the book  possibly not very nicely was a way of  ending this great visit with Alexander I  just wanted to read the last two  sentence as a way of also commending the  book to you and Alexander's gonna be  signing just after our chat here the  Supriya film is not a reality that we  can live with every minute of every day  but it holds and it's terrible 26  seconds a painful fundamental human  truth that will never grow old and that  every generation must grapple with for  itself  [Applause]  [Music]  and the final author you'll hear from  the San Antonio Book Festival is Lydia  pine she discusses the lesser-known  fossil discoveries that have changed our  understanding of evolution  good afternoon we're gonna go ahead and  get started we'd like to welcome you to  the skeletons in the closet session my  name is Jennifer Matthews I'm a  professor of anthropology at Trinity  University here in San Antonio and this  is my colleague Lydia pine and we want  to first of all say thank you for coming  to the session and we want to thank the  San Antonio Public Library as well as  the Southwest school of arts and crafts  for opening up their facilities for this  great event today and we are also  supposed to encourage everybody to  please post any of your photos your  tweets and things please put those up on  social media and use the hashtag si  Bookfest or si at si Book Festival for  Twitter so please put those out there  out into the
 

